  "articleBody": "It's super frustrating when you've got a new door ready to go and it just doesn't quite fit the opening. Often, this boils down to some common oversights during the framing process. A big one is confusing the nominal door size with the actual door and frame dimensions. You can't just measure the door slab; you've got to think about the entire prehung unit, including the jamb and the clearances needed for shims. If the rough opening isn't spot-on, you're going to struggle with alignment, how the door swings, and even whether it closes properly. We see this a lot, and it's usually an easy fix if caught early. Sometimes, though, the problem is deeper, especially if the original framing wasn't plumb, level, and square. You really want to check those measurements at multiple points in the opening, not just once. If you're finding yourself wrestling with a door that just won't cooperate, or if you suspect your wall might be load-bearing, it's probably time to call in some professional help. We can quickly assess what's going on and get that door opening framed correctly, ensuring a smooth, hassle-free installation. Don't waste your weekend fighting with a misaligned door; let us help you get it right.",
